// ScaleTestRunner — climb the generated ladder, measure its COST, print it, and record the trend.
//
// IT RENDERS NO VERDICT. There is nothing here to pass.
//
// `spec-test` proves the compiler is CORRECT and gates on it. This is an INSTRUMENT: it compiles a
// ladder of generated programs — six rungs, each double the last — measures ALLOCATIONS, FREES,
// BYTES and CPU TICKS per phase per rung, prints them, and appends a datapoint to
// `docs/optimization-log.md`. The question it answers is *"what has this compiler's cost actually
// done, change by change"*, not *"may I merge"*.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// NO WALL TIME, AND NO CURVES. Both deletions, and why.
// ===========================================================================================
//
// IT DOES NOT COLLECT WALL TIME, AND THAT HALF HAS NEVER CHANGED. Wall time counts every OTHER
// process on the machine too, so a dated table of it compares a loaded box in July against an idle
// one in August and concludes nothing. Measured, on this very ladder: one run read `phase:parse` at
// x5.03 then x1.78 across a DOUBLING corpus, which is not a curve of any shape — it is preemption.
// (The COMPILER still times itself, and should: `--metrics=<path>` and `--log=compiler:debug` give
// per-phase milliseconds for ONE compile, which is where a clock is worth reading and where nothing
// is trended against another machine on another day. This runner reads those metrics files and
// ignores their wall-clock column.)
//
// WHAT IT DOES COLLECT INSTEAD IS CPU TIME, and the distinction is the whole reason a cost column
// can be here at all. `__Builtins.threadCpuTicks()` advances only while the compiling THREAD is
// scheduled, so preemption and every other process are invisible to it. That makes it trendable
// where wall time is not — and it is the ONLY column that can see a cost which allocates nothing,
// the class every time-only quadratic in PLAN.md belongs to.
//
// ⚠ IT IS NOT EXACT, AND THE OTHER COLUMNS ARE. Allocations and bytes are bit-for-bit reproducible:
// the same compiler on the same corpus produces the same digits whatever else the machine is doing,
// so a number that MOVED, moved for a reason. `cpuTicks` still moves a few percent with turbo,
// thermal throttling and cache pressure from other cores, which is why each rung is compiled
// `--repeat` times and the MINIMUM is kept (see the repetition section below). Against the question
// the ladder asks — x2 linear vs x4 quadratic — that band has a 100% margin. Against a claimed
// few-percent constant-factor win it is worth nothing; use the allocation columns for that.
//
// AND IT FITS NOTHING. The ladder DOUBLES, so the ratio between two consecutive rungs IS the growth,
// read straight off the raw numbers — x2.00 is linear, x4.00 is quadratic. A fitted exponent could
// say nothing that the doubling had not already said, and it dragged a residual behind it, and the
// residual dragged a NOISY verdict, and the NOISY verdict got an exemption written for it so that it
// would stop complaining. That is optimizing the gauge instead of the engine, and it is only ever
// tempting when there is a gauge that can be wrong. A ratio is a division: there is no light to turn
// green, so a surprising number is a READING TO EXPLAIN. See ScaleRatio.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// WHAT AN EXIT CODE STILL MEANS
// ===========================================================================================
//
// Exit 0 on any successful run, whatever the numbers say. Non-zero ONLY when the run itself broke —
// it could not measure, or could not deliver what it measured:
//
//   - a rung would not compile,
//   - a metrics file was missing or malformed,
//   - the generated corpus was DEGENERATE — it folded away, so nothing was measured at all
//     (ScaleCorpus.assertCorpusCompiled),
//   - the trend log could not be read, or a `--note` could not honestly be recorded in it,
//   - a `--result-json` document was asked for and could not be written. The caller that asks for
//     that file is a MACHINE, and a run that answers it with an exit code of 0 and no file has told
//     it a successful-looking lie.
//
// A broken MEASUREMENT is a broken run. A SURPRISING NUMBER is not. The report says which happened,
// loudly, and never confuses the two.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// A SUBPROCESS PER RUNG, AND `--repeat` OF THEM
// ===========================================================================================
//
// shv2 has no on-disk cache — `Compiler.loadProject` reads every source fresh in every process — so
// a new process is COLD BY CONSTRUCTION and there is nothing to bust. Spawning also means a crash
// at the top rung kills that rung and not the run, which is not hypothetical: the 19,200-block
// shape at rung 5 is exactly where the historical SIGSEGV lived, and a runner that died with it
// would report nothing at all about the five rungs that had already climbed.
//
// EACH RUNG IS COMPILED `--repeat` TIMES (`DefaultRepeatCount` — ONE — unless asked otherwise), AND
// THE TWO KINDS OF COLUMN TAKE OPPOSITE THINGS FROM THAT. That asymmetry is why the default is 1 and
// why raising it is a real thing to do; see `DefaultRepeatCount` for the measurement behind both:
//
//   - MEMORY needs exactly ONE repetition and gains nothing whatever from a second. Allocations,
//     frees and bytes are bit-for-bit reproducible, so every repeat returns the same digits. They
//     are taken from the FIRST compile and never re-measured.
//   - CPU needs SEVERAL, and takes the MINIMUM. `cpuTicks` is the one measure here that is not
//     reproducible: it moves with turbo, thermal throttling and cache pressure, so one sample is
//     partly a reading of whatever the machine was doing during it. The minimum is the
//     least-disturbed run, which is the right estimator for a cost that noise can only ever ADD to.
//     A mean would be dragged by a single stall, and a stall is exactly the thing that is not the
//     compiler.
//
// WHEN THERE IS MORE THAN ONE REPEAT, they owe the memory columns something other than a second
// measurement: they are CROSS-CHECKED FOR EQUALITY. Every phase's allocs/frees/bytes, and the emitted
// code size, must be IDENTICAL in every repeat, and a difference is a BROKEN RUN rather than a number
// to average away — a compiler that allocates differently for the same input has made every row of
// the trend log suspect, and that log's whole claim is that a movement in those columns is real. The
// check costs nothing beyond the repeat itself, and only a repeated compile can make it.
//
// ⚠ AT THE DEFAULT OF 1 THIS CHECK DOES NOT RUN — there is nothing to compare against. That is the
// deliberate cost of the default (see `DefaultRepeatCount`), and `--repeat=2` is the cheapest way to
// buy it back. It is not the only line of defence: the fragment goldens prove byte-identical codegen
// across ~1,900 spec tests every suite run, which is a far stronger determinism gate — it simply does
// not look at allocation COUNTS, which is precisely the gap this check covered.

// A rung's metrics TSV and its executable, in the corpus ROOT — NEVER inside the rung's own source
// directory.
//
// That is a correctness requirement, not a filing preference. `build <dir>` LISTS the directory it
// is given, and every entry costs the compiler a `FilePath` and a `String`: a rung whose own output
// sits beside its sources measures a different program than the same rung on a fresh checkout. It
// used to, by exactly 26 allocations and 945 bytes per rung, and the "reproducible" memory numbers
// were reproducible only on a machine that had run the suite before. See ScaleCorpus's header, and
// `pruneRungDir`, which enforces the other half of the invariant.
let RungMetricsSuffix = ".metrics.tsv"
let RungOutputSuffix = ".out"

// The compiler must be given a RELATIVE rung path, and this is load-bearing rather than tidy.
//
// The byte counters count every String the compiler allocates, and a source file's PATH is one of
// them. An absolute path is as long as wherever the repo happens to live, so the same rung compiled
// from `C:/dev/maxon` and from `/home/e/src/maxon-checkout` would allocate a different number of
// BYTES. A relative path is the same string on every machine. The subprocess inherits this process's
// working directory, which is the repo root (see Main.resolveSpecDir, which relies on the same).
//
// ⚠ AND IT DOES NOT KEEP THE CHECKOUT OUT OF THE BYTE COLUMN, which this comment claimed until it was
// measured. The COMPILER resolves each source path against `Directory.currentPath()` itself — per file,
// per compile, in `Queries.isStdlibSourceFile` — so the working directory's length lands in the byte
// column whatever this side hands it. The reading that showed it is ScaleHistory's header, under (2),
// and it is deliberately NOT copied here: the copy that lived on this line said "against exactly 0
// allocations", which was the general rule an implementer then lost most of a session to, and a fourth
// site is a fourth place to leave saying the old thing. Keeping this relative still removes the ARGV
// copy of the path and is still right; the trend log's byte column is comparable across two runs only
// when the working directory matches too, which is why `mintedAtCell` records both.
let CorpusRelativeRoot = ".scale-tmp"

// Everything in the scale suite that can go wrong at RUN time: a rung that would not compile, a
// metrics file that never appeared, a trend log that could not be read.
//
// A union carrying its own message, because Maxon errors are enums/unions and a message is the only
// payload any of these needs — every one of them is reported to a human and none is recovered from
// programmatically. `scaleErrorText` is the single place it is unwrapped.
export union ScaleError implements Error
	reason(message String)
end 'ScaleError'

export function scaleErrorText(error ScaleError) returns String
	return match error 'e'
		reason(message) gives message
	end 'e'
end 'scaleErrorText'

// One phase's numbers, as parsed back out of a rung's metrics TSV.
//
// STILL NO `nanos` FIELD, and now for a sharper reason than "time is unreliable". The TSV carries
// TWO cost columns and they are not the same kind of number. Wall time counts every other process
// on the box — carried into a dated trend table it would compare a loaded machine in July against
// an idle one in August, which is the argument this suite was built on and it has not changed.
// `cpuTicks` counts only the compiling thread: preemption is invisible to it, so it survives a
// busy machine and can be trended. One is read, one is skipped, and the difference is which
// question each can answer.
//
// ⚠ `cpuTicks` IS NOT BIT-REPRODUCIBLE the way allocs/frees/bytes are. It still moves with turbo,
// thermal throttling and cache pressure from other cores — a few percent, against the x2-vs-x4
// signal the doubling ladder reads, which has a 100% margin. So: a movement in the memory columns
// is always real; a movement in this one is real only outside the noise band. See the trust table
// in docs/optimization-log.md, which states the band that was actually measured.
export type PhaseMetric
	export let allocs as AllocCount
	export let frees as AllocCount
	export let bytes as ByteCount
	export let cpuTicks as CpuTicks

	export static function create(allocs AllocCount, frees AllocCount, bytes ByteCount, cpuTicks CpuTicks) returns PhaseMetric
		return Self{allocs: allocs, frees: frees, bytes: bytes, cpuTicks: cpuTicks}
	end 'create'

	export static function zero() returns PhaseMetric
		return Self{allocs: 0, frees: 0, bytes: 0, cpuTicks: 0}
	end 'zero'
end 'PhaseMetric'

export typealias PhaseMetricArray = Array with PhaseMetric

// Everything one rung reported. `phases` and `regAlloc` are dense, indexed by their enum's ordinal,
// so a phase that failed to appear in the TSV is a parse ERROR rather than a silent zero — see
// `parseRungMetrics`.
export type RungResult
	export let rung as RungIndex
	export let sourceBytes as SourceByteCount
	export let codeBytes as CodeByteCount
	export let total as PhaseMetric
	export let phases as PhaseMetricArray
	export let regAlloc as PhaseMetricArray

	export static function create(rung RungIndex, sourceBytes SourceByteCount, codeBytes CodeByteCount, total PhaseMetric, phases PhaseMetricArray, regAlloc PhaseMetricArray) returns RungResult
		return Self{rung: rung, sourceBytes: sourceBytes, codeBytes: codeBytes, total: total, phases: phases, regAlloc: regAlloc}
	end 'create'

	export function phaseAt(phase CompilePhase) returns PhaseMetric
		return try self.phases.get(phase.ordinal) otherwise panic("RungResult.phaseAt: phases.get OOB for '{phase.name}'")
	end 'phaseAt'

	export function regAllocAt(sub RegAllocPhase) returns PhaseMetric
		return try self.regAlloc.get(sub.ordinal) otherwise panic("RungResult.regAllocAt: regAlloc.get OOB for '{sub.name}'")
	end 'regAllocAt'
end 'RungResult'

export typealias RungResultArray = Array with RungResult

// ===========================================================================================
// The phases this instrument reports, enumerated ONCE
// ===========================================================================================

// WHAT one row of the per-phase table is measured from: a compile phase, or one of the register
// allocator's sub-phases.
//
// The sub-phases are a BREAKDOWN of `phase:regalloc` and not extra phases beside it — adding them to
// the same list would double-count every allocation the allocator makes — so they are enumerated
// after the phases and printed under them.
//
// ONE ENUMERATION, walked by the human table and by `--result-json` alike. Two hand-maintained walks
// of the same enums is how a phase gets added to `CompilePhase`, timed, metered, and then silently
// omitted from one output but not the other; and a name built at two sites is a name that can differ
// at two sites. The list, and the naming, live here.
export union PhaseSource
	compilePhase(phase CompilePhase)
	regAllocPhase(sub RegAllocPhase)
end 'PhaseSource'

export typealias PhaseSourceArray = Array with PhaseSource

let PhaseNamePrefix = "phase:"
let RegAllocNamePrefix = "regalloc:"

export function scalePhaseSources() returns PhaseSourceArray
	var sources = PhaseSourceArray.create()

	for phase in CompilePhase.allCases 'eachPhase'
		sources.push(PhaseSource.compilePhase(phase))
	end 'eachPhase'

	for sub in RegAllocPhase.allCases 'eachSub'
		sources.push(PhaseSource.regAllocPhase(sub))
	end 'eachSub'

	return sources
end 'scalePhaseSources'

export function phaseSourceName(source PhaseSource) returns String
	return match source 'src'
		compilePhase(phase) gives "{PhaseNamePrefix}{phase.name}"
		regAllocPhase(sub) gives "{RegAllocNamePrefix}{sub.name}"
	end 'src'
end 'phaseSourceName'

export function phaseMetricOf(source PhaseSource, result RungResult) returns PhaseMetric
	return match source 'src'
		compilePhase(phase) gives result.phaseAt(phase)
		regAllocPhase(sub) gives result.regAllocAt(sub)
	end 'src'
end 'phaseMetricOf'

// ===========================================================================================
// `--repeat=<n>` — how many times each rung is compiled
// ===========================================================================================

// How many compiles one rung gets. The upper bound is a TYPO GUARD and nothing deeper: the cost is
// LINEAR in it, unlike `--rungs`, where each step doubles the program and the bound is protecting
// you from a cliff. But `--repeat=300` on a six-rung ladder is an hour of compiling that nobody
// meant to ask for, and a minimum stops improving long before that.
//
// The alias's range IS the accepted range, so the type and `parseRepeatCount`'s bound check cannot
// disagree about what a valid repeat count is.
export typealias RepeatCount = int(1 to 25)

export let MinRepeatCount = 1 as RepeatCount
export let MaxRepeatCount = 25 as RepeatCount

// ONE (user ruling, 2026-07-27) — because the routine per-rung read is a MEMORY read, and MEASURED,
// the repeats buy that read NOTHING.
//
// This was THREE, on a judgement about noise: one sample cannot tell a stall from a cost, two cannot
// tell which of a disagreeing pair was disturbed, three is where a minimum starts being stable. That
// reasoning is still correct ABOUT THE CPU COLUMN. What it got wrong is who pays: it is charged to
// every rung of every run, while the column it defends is the one column this instrument reads
// second.
//
// Measured on the same tree, `--repeat=3` vs `--repeat=1`:
//   - the per-phase ALLOCATION tables are BYTE-IDENTICAL. Not close — identical, as the section
//     above already promised they would be. The default was buying the memory columns nothing.
//   - the run costs 51 s vs 17 s. Exactly 3x, because the cost is linear in this number.
//   - the CPU column is where it shows: a single sample reads ~+9-10% higher at rung 5 (lex +9.7%,
//     parse +10.2%, regalloc +8.9%), and per-phase exponents wobble up to +-0.5 (pruneDeadBlockArgs
//     1.91 -> 2.41, semanticCheck 1.85 -> 2.32, runtimeAugment 1.95 -> 1.11).
//
// Against the only question a DOUBLING ladder asks — x2 is linear, x4 is quadratic — a +-0.5 wobble
// still leaves 100% of margin, which is why 1 is enough for the routine read.
//
// ⚠ RAISE IT WHEN YOU ARE COMPARING TWO BINARIES' CPU. An A/B is exactly the case the minimum was
// built for: there the effect under test can be a few percent, so a single sample cannot carry it.
// (`--repeat=3` and `--repeat=5` are what the 2026-07-28 `phase:signatures` investigation ran, and
// interleaving — not sample count alone — is what finally settled it.)
//
// ⚠ TWO THINGS ARE GENUINELY GIVEN UP HERE, and neither is hidden:
//   1. The cross-repeat EQUALITY CHECK below cannot run at 1 — a repeated compile is the only thing
//      that can make it. Codegen determinism is still gated, far more strongly, by the fragment
//      goldens over ~1,900 spec tests; what goes unchecked is ALLOCATION-COUNT determinism, which
//      those goldens do not see. `--repeat=2` restores the check for anyone who wants it.
//   2. CPU rows logged before this date are MINIMA and rows after it are single samples, so they sit
//      ~10% apart for no reason in the compiler. The changeover is recorded in
//      docs/optimization-log.md at the date; do not read that step as a regression.
export let DefaultRepeatCount = 1 as RepeatCount

// The outcome of parsing `--repeat=<n>`. A union rather than a throw, for `--rungs`'s reason: the
// caller is inside the argument loop and must neither diverge nor invent a value — `invalid` is
// recorded as a bad option and the driver then refuses to run at all.
export union RepeatCountArg
	valid(count RepeatCount)
	invalid
end 'RepeatCountArg'

// Parse `--repeat=<n>`.
//
// REFUSED, never clamped — the same door as `--rungs` and `--workers`. `--repeat=0` asks for a rung
// that is never compiled, and a caller who typed it and silently got 3 would believe they had
// measured something they had not.
//
// The digits are `parseCount`'s job, as they are for `--rungs`; this decides only what a valid RANGE
// is, and keeps that decision in the same function as the narrowing cast so the two cannot drift.
export function parseRepeatCount(raw String) returns RepeatCountArg
	let value = try parseCount(raw) otherwise 'notANumber'
		return RepeatCountArg.invalid
	end 'notANumber'

	if value < MinRepeatCount or value > MaxRepeatCount 'outOfRange'
		return RepeatCountArg.invalid
	end 'outOfRange'
	return RepeatCountArg.valid(value as RepeatCount)
end 'parseRepeatCount'

// ===========================================================================================
// Entry point
// ===========================================================================================

// The command, wrapped so the checkout's TREE LOCK is taken for its whole duration and released
// through ONE exit — `measureScaleLadder` below has a dozen returns, and a release written at each of
// them is a release the next one forgets (see `Main.compileAndReport`).
//
// A scale run compiles a generated ladder with the in-tree binary and writes its corpus into the
// checkout, so it contends with a `build` (which replaces that binary mid-ladder) and with a suite
// (which is the other thing sizing the machine) exactly as those two contend with each other. And its
// numbers are the reason it matters most: a rung measured against a machine running somebody else's
// suite is a recorded row of noise, and a recorded row is permanent.
export function runScaleTest(args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	if refusedPositionalBeyond(args, allowed: 0, usage: ScaleTestUsage) 'noPositional'
		return 1
	end 'noPositional'

	let corpusRoot = try corpusRootPath(args) otherwise 'badCorpus'
		return brokenRun("the corpus root is not a valid path", detail: "--emit-corpus={args.emitCorpus}")
	end 'badCorpus'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `--per-type` TRACE COMPILER IS BUILT HERE, BEFORE THE LOCK, AND THAT ORDER IS THE FIX.**
	// Only the BOOTSTRAP can build it (shv2 cannot compile itself), and the bootstrap takes this
	// checkout's tree lock for EVERY directory build — unconditionally, because it has no `-o` and so no
	// way to say the caller owns the output. That `-o` escape (`Main.acquireBuildTreeLock`) is what makes
	// every OTHER nested build in this harness a non-event: the rung compiles pass one, the per-type
	// rung re-compiles pass one, and none of them contend. The trace build is the single exception, and
	// asking for it from inside the locked run refused it EVERY TIME — the pass has never run since the
	// lock landed, and it reported that as `SKIPPED` while the run exited 0.
	//
	// ⛔ **RE-ENTRANCY WAS THE OTHER CANDIDATE AND IS THE WRONG TRADE HERE.** It would mean a holder
	// handing its token to a child through the environment and BOTH lock implementations honouring it —
	// `maxon-sharp/TreeLock.cs`, `Compiler/TreeLock.maxon` and the `TreeLockParity` gate that keeps the
	// two identical. Three files and a C# build, to add an exception to a safety mechanism, when the
	// dependency is simply in the wrong order: nothing about building the trace compiler needs the
	// ladder's lock, because it writes `.scale-tmp/mmtrace-src/.maxon/` and nothing else writes that.
	//
	// ⚠ What this does NOT protect is the source COPY, which now happens before the lock is taken. The
	// build that follows it is covered — the bootstrap child takes the tree lock itself for its whole
	// duration — so the exposed window is the copy alone, and a second `scale-test` racing it is refused
	// at the acquire below exactly as it is today. That is the residual, and it is stated rather than
	// left for a reader to find.
	var tracer = TracePreparation.notRequested
	if args.perType 'prepareTracer'
		let traceExe = try buildTraceCompiler(corpusRoot) otherwise (error) 'tracerFailed'
			return brokenRun("--per-type was requested and its trace compiler could not be built", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'tracerFailed'
		tracer = TracePreparation.ready(traceExe)
	end 'prepareTracer'

	let lockExit = acquireTreeLock(corpusRoot)
	if lockExit != 0 'busyTree'
		return lockExit
	end 'busyTree'

	let runExit = measureScaleLadder(args, corpusRoot: corpusRoot, tracer: tracer)
	releaseTreeLock()
	return runExit
end 'runScaleTest'

// `scale-test` reads NO positional argument, so any of them is a typo the run must not swallow — it
// took several minutes and produced a table about something the caller did not ask for. Spelled with
// the shared helper rather than a second copy of the message, at the ONE count that differs.
let ScaleTestUsage = "maxon-shv2 scale-test [--rungs=<n>] [--repeat=<n>] [--per-type] [--note=<text>] [--emit-corpus=<dir>] [--result-json=<path>]"

// Whether `--per-type` was asked for, and if so the compiler that will serve it — built before the
// lock and carried through the run. A union rather than an empty `FilePath`, because there is no path
// value meaning "not requested" and a sentinel one would be indistinguishable from a build that
// silently produced nothing.
union TracePreparation
	notRequested
	ready(exe FilePath)
end 'TracePreparation'

function measureScaleLadder(args MaxonArgs, corpusRoot FilePath, tracer TracePreparation) returns ExitCode
	// `--update-required` is GONE from this command, and saying so is worth four lines. It rewrote
	// committed memory goldens; there are no goldens. It is still a spec-test flag, and the driver
	// parses the command line before it knows which command is running — so without this it would be
	// accepted here and silently do nothing, on a command line every document in this tree used to
	// tell people to type.
	if args.updateRequired 'noGoldens'
		printError("scale-test: --update-required is gone. It rewrote the committed memory goldens, and there are none:\n")
		printError("            this suite renders no verdict and has nothing to accept.\n")
		printError("            To RECORD a run in {OptimizationLogRelativePath}, use --note=\"<what changed and why>\".\n")
		return 1
	end 'noGoldens'

	// WHERE THIS RUN IS MEASURED is resolved FIRST, because it is two things at once: what each rung is
	// compiled BY and FROM, and the provenance every recorded row carries.
	//
	// BOTH PATHS, and neither is optional. A compile derives every stdlib source path from the compiler's
	// own executable path, and it resolves every RUNG source path against the working directory
	// (`Queries.isStdlibSourceFile`, per file) — so the BYTE column of this run is comparable only with a
	// logged row that had the same two. Keyed on the executable alone, one binary run from a second
	// directory read `comparable` and printed a +203,310-byte fiction at rung 5. See ScaleHistory's header
	// for both measurements.
	let compilerExe = try Process.executablePath() otherwise 'noExe'
		return brokenRun("the running compiler's own path could not be determined", detail: "Process.executablePath() failed")
	end 'noExe'

	// ⚠ AND WHETHER THAT BINARY IS OLDER THAN THE TREE IT WAS BUILT FROM, before a rung is generated.
	// A ladder read off the previous binary is what filed this check: a build failed `error-codes
	// check`, its exit code went unread, and a whole run's numbers were attributed to a change that
	// was not in the binary that produced them. A recorded row is permanent, so the wrong provenance
	// outlives the session that made it — see ScaleHistory on why every row states where it was
	// measured. This is the same question one level up: WHAT was measured.
	let freshnessExit = refuseIfStale(compilerExe, role: "the scale-test compiler")
	if freshnessExit != 0 'staleBinary'
		return freshnessExit
	end 'staleBinary'

	// Resolved to an absolute path for the comparison's sake, not for the compiler's: the compiler is
	// still handed the RELATIVE rung path (see `CorpusRelativeRoot`), and this is the string that says
	// which directory that relative path was relative TO.
	let mintedAt = mintedAtCell(compilerExe.toString(), corpusRoot: corpusRoot.resolve(Directory.currentPath()).toString())

	// The trend is read next, before a single rung is compiled. It is what this run's numbers are
	// reported against, and if a `--note` cannot honestly be recorded in it, saying so after thirty
	// seconds of compiling would be a rude way to report a typo.
	let trend = try loadScaleTrend() otherwise (error) 'noTrend'
		return brokenRun("the trend log could not be read", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'noTrend'

	if not args.note.isEmpty() 'wantsToRecord'
		try assertRecordable(trend, note: args.note, mintedAt: mintedAt, rungs: args.rungs) otherwise (error) 'unrecordable'
			return brokenRun("this run could not be recorded", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'unrecordable'
	end 'wantsToRecord'

	// Whether this run's BYTES may be subtracted from the logged row's. Decided ONCE, here, and handed
	// to every consumer of the comparison — the human report and `--result-json` alike — so the two
	// cannot come to different conclusions about the same pair of rows.
	let bytesComparable = byteComparability(trend, mintedAt: mintedAt)

	print("scale-test: generating {args.rungs} rung(s) under {corpusRoot.toString()}, compiling each {args.repeat} time(s)\n")
	let ladder = try generateCorpus(corpusRoot, rungs: args.rungs) otherwise (error) 'genErr'
		return brokenRun("the corpus could not be generated", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'genErr'

	// SAY WHAT THIS CORPUS COVERS AND WHAT IT CANNOT, on every run and before any number. An instrument
	// that cannot state its blind spots produces confident zeros — see `printCorpusManifest`.
	printCorpusManifest()

	if not args.emitCorpus.isEmpty() 'corpusOnly'
		print("scale-test: corpus written to {corpusRoot.toString()} (--emit-corpus: not compiling)\n")
		return 0
	end 'corpusOnly'

	// AND SAY WHAT THE COMPILER'S OWN INPUT WAS — the corpus is only half of what each rung compiles.
	// Asked here rather than beside the manifest above because it describes the COMPILES, and the
	// `--emit-corpus` run just above this line makes none.
	try printStdlibWalkCensus() otherwise (error) 'noStdlibWalk'
		return brokenRun("the checkout's stdlib/ could not be walked", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'noStdlibWalk'

	printRungHeader()
	var results = RungResultArray.create()
	for rung in ladder 'eachRung'
		// One rung compiled is this run's unit of progress, and the tree lock's heartbeat is what
		// says the holder is still making it. The compiles themselves are CHILD processes, so none
		// of the compiler's own phase heartbeats reach this record — the ladder has to say so itself.
		touchTreeLock()

		var result = try compileRung(compilerExe, root: corpusRoot, rung: rung) otherwise (error) 'rungFailed'
			// A rung that would not compile — or that compiled and then DISAGREED with the generator about
			// what it computes — is a broken run, not a slow one: every later rung is a superset of this
			// one's shapes, so there is nothing left to measure and nothing the rungs below it can be
			// compared against.
			//
			// ⚠ THE HEADLINE SAYS "did not deliver a rung to measure" AND NOT "failed to compile", because
			// since BATCH10 this arm has two causes and naming only the first was a lie about the second:
			// a rung that compiles cleanly and then exits with a failed witness reported itself as a
			// COMPILE failure, with an empty compiler stderr underneath it to prove it had not. The detail
			// line is where the two are told apart, and it always names which happened.
			return brokenRun("rung {rung.index} did not deliver a rung to measure", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'rungFailed'

		// The repeats. The first compile above is repeat 1, so this loop runs `repeat - 1` more times
		// and `--repeat=1` skips it entirely — one path, not a special case. Each fold keeps the first
		// compile's memory, takes the least CPU seen so far, and refuses a memory number that moved.
		for run in 1 upto args.repeat 'eachRepeat'
			let again = try compileRung(compilerExe, root: corpusRoot, rung: rung) otherwise (error) 'repeatFailed'
				return brokenRun("rung {rung.index} compiled once and then FAILED on repeat {run + 1}", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
			end 'repeatFailed'

			result = try leastCpuRung(result, again: again, run: run) otherwise (error) 'nondeterministic'
				return brokenRun("the compiler is NONDETERMINISTIC — two identical compiles of rung {rung.index} reported different MEMORY, so every allocation and byte in {OptimizationLogRelativePath} is suspect", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
			end 'nondeterministic'
		end 'eachRepeat'

		printRungLine(result, results: results)
		results.push(result)
	end 'eachRung'

	// Check the instrument before the subject. A corpus that folded away measured NOTHING, and every
	// number below it would be a number about nothing — a beautifully proportional set of them.
	try assertCorpusCompiled(results) otherwise (error) 'degenerate'
		return brokenRun("the generated corpus was DEGENERATE, so this run measured nothing", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'degenerate'

	printPhaseTable(results, measure: ScaleMeasure.allocations, repeat: args.repeat)
	printPhaseTable(results, measure: ScaleMeasure.bytes, repeat: args.repeat)
	printPhaseTable(results, measure: ScaleMeasure.cpuTicks, repeat: args.repeat)

	// DID ANYTHING MOVE — decided ONCE, like the comparability above it, and for the same reason: the
	// report's closing sentence and the recording sentence are two statements of one fact, and two
	// answers to one question is how they come to contradict each other in the output of one run.
	let memoryMoved = anyMemoryMoved(trend, results: results, bytes: bytesComparable)

	printMemoryTrend(results, trend: trend, mintedAt: mintedAt, bytes: bytesComparable, moved: memoryMoved)
	printCorpusGrowth(results)

	if not args.resultJson.isEmpty() 'json'
		try writeResultJson(args.resultJson, results: results, trend: trend, repeat: RepeatSampling.stated(args.repeat), mintedAt: mintedAt, bytes: bytesComparable) otherwise (error) 'jsonFailed'
			return brokenRun("the result document could not be written", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'jsonFailed'
	end 'json'

	if not args.note.isEmpty() 'record'
		let recorded = recordRun(results, note: args.note, mintedAt: mintedAt, bytes: bytesComparable, moved: memoryMoved)
		if recorded != 0 'recordFailed'
			return recorded
		end 'recordFailed'
	end 'record'

	return match tracer 'perTypePass'
		notRequested gives 0
		ready(traceExe) gives runPerTypePass(results, traceExe: traceExe, corpusRoot: corpusRoot)
	end 'perTypePass'
end 'measureScaleLadder'

// The ONE way this instrument fails: the RUN broke — it could not measure, or could not deliver what
// it measured. It says so in those words, so that a reader can never mistake it for a judgement about
// the numbers; there is no judgement about the numbers.
//
// Each caller names what broke, and the causes are not interchangeable: a degenerate corpus measured
// NOTHING, while a result document that could not be written measured everything and could not hand
// it over. This line therefore says "the run", which is true of both, rather than "nothing was
// measured", which was written for the first and is a lie about the second.
export function brokenRun(what String, detail String) returns ExitCode
	printError("\nscale-test: BROKEN RUN — {what}.\n")
	printError("  {detail}\n")
	printError("  The RUN failed. This is not a verdict about the compiler's numbers. Fix it and re-run.\n")
	return 1
end 'brokenRun'

// Record this run in the trend log.
//
// `--note` IS AN INSTRUCTION, NOT A REQUEST. If it was given, the row is written — full stop.
//
// This used to decline when allocations and bytes both matched the last logged row, on the theory
// that a row about nothing is noise. It is the wrong theory, and it fails SILENTLY, which is the
// worse half: the operator who typed `--note` asking for the datapoint got a line of prose and no
// row. A tool that second-guesses an explicit instruction from its operator, and then leaves the
// history it was told to write simply absent, is worse than one that logs a dull row — and the
// operator is the only party who knows why the run was made.
//
// The REPORT stays honest — an unmoved run is told it did not move — but the row is written.
//
// (Every guard that protects the log's INTEGRITY still refuses: a note carrying a `|` or a forged
// marker, a duplicate marker, a ladder of the wrong width, a curve set that is not this compiler's.
// Those reject BAD INPUT, which is a different thing entirely from overruling a valid one.)
function recordRun(results RungResultArray, note String, mintedAt String, bytes ByteDelta, moved bool) returns ExitCode
	if not moved 'unchanged'
		print("\nscale-test: {unchangedColumnsText(bytes)} since the last logged run — recording anyway, because --note was given.\n")
	end 'unchanged'

	try recordScaleRun(results, note: note, mintedAt: mintedAt) otherwise (error) 'writeFailed'
		return brokenRun("the run could not be recorded in the trend log", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'writeFailed'
	return 0
end 'recordRun'

// WHICH columns that "nothing moved" is a claim about. When the byte delta is withheld the sentence may
// not say bytes are unchanged: nothing compared them, and reporting silence as agreement is the same
// failure as reporting a path artifact as a regression, pointed the other way.
function unchangedColumnsText(bytes ByteDelta) returns String
	return match bytes 'b'
		comparable gives "no change in allocations or bytes"
		withheld gives "no change in allocations (bytes not compared)"
	end 'b'
end 'unchangedColumnsText'

// Where the corpus is generated. `--emit-corpus=<dir>` puts it somewhere a human can read it;
// otherwise it goes to the gitignored `.scale-tmp/`, relative to the working directory — see
// CorpusRelativeRoot for why relative rather than absolute is load-bearing.
function corpusRootPath(args MaxonArgs) returns FilePath throws FilePathError
	if not args.emitCorpus.isEmpty() 'explicit'
		return try FilePath.from(args.emitCorpus)
	end 'explicit'
	return try FilePath.from(CorpusRelativeRoot)
end 'corpusRootPath'

// ===========================================================================================
// Compiling one rung
// ===========================================================================================

function compileRung(compilerExe FilePath, root FilePath, rung ScaleRung) returns RungResult throws ScaleError
	let metricsPath = try corpusRootFile(root, name: "{RungDirPrefix}{rung.index}{RungMetricsSuffix}")
	let outputBase = try corpusRootFile(root, name: "{RungDirPrefix}{rung.index}{RungOutputSuffix}")

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(BuildSubcommandName)
	argv.push(rung.dir.toString())
	argv.push(OutputFlagName)
	argv.push(outputBase.toString())
	argv.push("{MetricsFlagPrefix}{metricsPath.toString()}")

	let compile = try runProcess(Executable.path(compilerExe), argv: argv, workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("compiler spawn failed: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	if compile.exitCode != 0 'compileFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("compiler exited {compile.exitCode}: {compile.stderr.trim()}")
	end 'compileFailed'

	try runRung(outputBase, rung: rung)

	// A MISSING metrics file is a failed rung, never a rung of zeroes. Zeroes would fit a curve —
	// a beautifully flat one — and report that the compiler had become infinitely fast.
	let text = try File.readText(metricsPath) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.index} produced no metrics file at {metricsPath.toString()}")

	return try parseRungMetrics(text, rung: rung)
end 'compileRung'

// ⭐⭐ **THE RUNG IS RUN, AND ITS EXIT CODE IS THE ONE THING THIS INSTRUMENT HAS EVER ASSERTED ABOUT
// WHAT IT COMPILES.** Until BATCH10 `compileRung` wrote an executable and never opened it: `outputBase`
// was passed to `-o` and never referenced again. So the ladder measured what compiling a program COST
// and had no opinion whatever on whether that program was correct — and it wasn't. MEASURED on rung 0
// before this: the accumulator wrapped i64 by the seventh driver, which turned `ownedSiteBlock`'s
// `if acc > 0` false and SKIPPED every heap-creating site in the corpus, and rung 1 did not terminate.
// Neither could be seen from a metrics file.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT A GATE AND DOES NOT BECOME ONE.** A wrong exit code is a BROKEN RUN — the same channel
// a rung that fails to compile already uses, and for the same reason: the instrument did not measure a
// surprising number, it measured a program that is not the program this generator describes, so there is
// nothing to read. There is still no verdict about any number here, and `scale-test` still exits 0
// whatever the numbers say.
//
// The witnesses themselves are `ScaleCorpus`'s (`driverWitnessTail`); this end only has to spawn the
// binary and insist on `CorpusOkExit`.
//
// ⚠ The host's executable suffix comes from `Target.hostExecutableExtension` — the compiler's OWN
// target model, spelled once for the whole tree. It was written out here as
// `executableExtension(detectHostTarget())` behind a comment saying `ScalePerType` and `SpecTestRunner`
// each already had a wrapper so a third would be duplication. **`SpecTestRunner` never had one** — it
// asks `executableExtension(effectiveTarget)`, which is the TARGET's extension and a different
// question — so the count was two spellings, not three, and the reason for keeping them apart was a
// claim about a function that does not exist (BATCH10 review).
function runRung(outputBase FilePath, rung ScaleRung) throws ScaleError
	let executable = try FilePath.from("{outputBase.toString()}{hostExecutableExtension()}") otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.index}'s output path is not a valid executable path")

	let run = try runProcess(Executable.path(executable), argv: StringArray.create(), workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.index} compiled but its binary {executable.toString()} could not be started: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	if run.exitCode != CorpusOkExit 'wrongExit'
		throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.index} exited {run.exitCode} where every witness holding is {CorpusOkExit}. Each generated driver hands `main` its argument back only when the construct it emitted computed what ScaleCorpus built it to compute, so this rung's binary DISAGREES with the generator about what its own program does — the numbers this rung produced are about a program that is not the one described. stderr: {run.stderr.trim()}")
	end 'wrongExit'
end 'runRung'

// A file in the corpus root. `join` is overloaded on String and FilePath and a bare String constant
// matches both, so the component is bound to a FilePath first.
function corpusRootFile(root FilePath, name String) returns FilePath throws ScaleError
	let component = try FilePath.from(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{name}' is not a valid path component")
	return root.join(component)
end 'corpusRootFile'

// ===========================================================================================
// Folding the repeats — MIN on the CPU column, EQUALITY on the memory ones
// ===========================================================================================

// This rung as measured so far, folded with one more compile of it.
//
// The result keeps the FIRST compile's memory and the LEAST CPU any compile has reported for each
// phase. `sourceBytes` is not compared because it cannot differ: it comes from the generated rung,
// not from the compile, and both compiles were handed the same ScaleRung.
//
// ⚠ EVERY CPU COLUMN TAKES ITS OWN MINIMUM, so the printed table can be a COMPOSITE of several
// compiles and the `total` row is not the sum of the phase rows. It never was — `unattributed` is a
// real gap between them — but this is a second reason, and it is the deliberate price of removing a
// stall from the ONE phase it landed in instead of discarding every good reading in the run around
// it. Each row is the best available answer to "what does this phase cost", which is the question
// the table is asked; no row is a claim about a particular process.
function leastCpuRung(first RungResult, again RungResult, run RepeatCount) returns RungResult throws ScaleError
	// The emitted code size is the degeneracy check's subject, so a rung that emitted a different
	// executable on the second compile is the same failure as a phase that allocated differently —
	// it is simply not a number this instrument may keep.
	if again.codeBytes != first.codeBytes 'codeMoved'
		throw ScaleError.reason("rung {first.rung} emitted {first.codeBytes} bytes of code on the first compile and {again.codeBytes} on repeat {run + 1}")
	end 'codeMoved'

	let total = try leastCpuPhase(first.total, again: again.total, what: MetricsKindTotal, rung: first.rung, run: run)

	// Walked off `scalePhaseSources` rather than off the two enums again, for the reason that list
	// exists: the fold then covers exactly what the tables print, and a phase cannot be added to one
	// walk and left out of the other. It yields both enums in ordinal order, so pushing in its order
	// rebuilds the dense ordinal indexing `RungResult.phaseAt`/`regAllocAt` read back.
	var phases = PhaseMetricArray.create()
	var regAlloc = PhaseMetricArray.create()
	for source in scalePhaseSources() 'eachSource'
		let least = try leastCpuPhase(phaseMetricOf(source, result: first), again: phaseMetricOf(source, result: again), what: phaseSourceName(source), rung: first.rung, run: run)
		match source 'dest'
			compilePhase then phases.push(least)
			regAllocPhase then regAlloc.push(least)
		end 'dest'
	end 'eachSource'

	return RungResult.create(first.rung, sourceBytes: first.sourceBytes, codeBytes: first.codeBytes, total: total, phases: phases, regAlloc: regAlloc)
end 'leastCpuRung'

// One phase, folded across two compiles: the memory triple must MATCH, and the CPU reading is the
// smaller of the two.
//
// MIN, NOT MEAN, and the asymmetry is the justification. Noise on a CPU reading can only ADD — a
// stall, a migration, a cache line a neighbouring core evicted — so the smallest sample is the one
// closest to what the phase costs, and every larger one carries something that is not the compiler.
// A mean has no such property: one stall moves it, and it moves it in the direction that reads as a
// regression.
//
// The memory numbers are not folded at all. They are bit-for-bit reproducible, so a second compile
// has nothing to add to them and everything to say about whether that claim is still true — which is
// the one thing a repeated compile can check for free, and the one-shot runner could not check at all.
function leastCpuPhase(first PhaseMetric, again PhaseMetric, what String, rung RungIndex, run RepeatCount) returns PhaseMetric throws ScaleError
	if again.allocs != first.allocs or again.frees != first.frees or again.bytes != first.bytes 'memoryMoved'
		throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung} `{what}`: the first compile reported {first.allocs} allocs / {first.frees} frees / {first.bytes} bytes, repeat {run + 1} reported {again.allocs} / {again.frees} / {again.bytes}")
	end 'memoryMoved'

	if again.cpuTicks >= first.cpuTicks 'noQuicker'
		return first
	end 'noQuicker'

	return PhaseMetric.create(first.allocs, frees: first.frees, bytes: first.bytes, cpuTicks: again.cpuTicks)
end 'leastCpuPhase'

// ===========================================================================================
// Parsing the metrics TSV (the format is MetricsEmit's; see its header)
// ===========================================================================================

function parseRungMetrics(text String, rung ScaleRung) returns RungResult throws ScaleError
	// SEEDED BY PUSHING, not by `resize`. `resize` on an array of STRUCTS sets the length
	// without giving the new slots storage: `count()` answers 5 while `get(0)` reports OOB.
	// This code survived it only because every slot is `set` before anything reads it — and
	// the identical line in CompileTimings.create, where the first access IS a read, panicked
	// on phase 0 the moment its column became a struct. Pushing is correct at both.
	var phases = PhaseMetricArray.create()
	var regAlloc = PhaseMetricArray.create()
	for _ in 0 upto CompilePhase.allCases.count() 'seedPhases'
		phases.push(PhaseMetric.zero())
	end 'seedPhases'
	for _ in 0 upto RegAllocPhase.allCases.count() 'seedRegAlloc'
		regAlloc.push(PhaseMetric.zero())
	end 'seedRegAlloc'

	// `seen` is what turns a missing row into an error instead of a zero. A dense array seeded with
	// zeroes cannot otherwise tell "this phase did no work" from "this phase never reported".
	var phaseSeen = BoolArray.create()
	phaseSeen.resize(CompilePhase.allCases.count())

	var total = PhaseMetric.zero()
	var totalSeen = false
	var codeBytes = 0 as CodeByteCount
	var codeSeen = false

	for line in text.split(MetricsRowSep) 'eachLine'
		let trimmed = line.trim()
		if trimmed.isEmpty() or trimmed.startsWith(MetricsCommentPrefix) 'skip'
			continue
		end 'skip'

		let fields = trimmed.split(MetricsColumnSep)
		if fields.count() != MetricsFieldCount 'malformed'
			throw ScaleError.reason("malformed metrics row (expected {MetricsFieldCount} tab-separated fields, got {fields.count()}): {trimmed}")
		end 'malformed'

		let kind = try fields.get(MetricsKindField) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: kind field OOB after count check")
		let name = try fields.get(MetricsNameField) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: name field OOB after count check")
		let metric = try parseMetricFields(fields)

		if kind == MetricsKindTotal 'isTotal'
			total = metric
			totalSeen = true
		end 'isTotal' else if kind == MetricsKindPhase 'isPhase'
			let phase = try CompilePhase.fromName(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("metrics names an unknown phase '{name}'")
			try phases.set(phase.ordinal, value: metric) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: phases.set OOB for '{name}'")
			try phaseSeen.set(phase.ordinal, value: true) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: phaseSeen.set OOB for '{name}'")
		end 'isPhase' else if kind == MetricsKindRegAlloc 'isRegAlloc'
			let sub = try RegAllocPhase.fromName(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("metrics names an unknown regalloc sub-phase '{name}'")
			try regAlloc.set(sub.ordinal, value: metric) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: regAlloc.set OOB for '{name}'")
		end 'isRegAlloc' else if kind == MetricsKindCode 'isCode'
			codeBytes = metric.bytes as CodeByteCount
			codeSeen = true
		end 'isCode' else if kind == MetricsKindUnattributed 'isUnattributed'
			// Read and discarded: the compiler emits it so the two reconciliation panics run on
			// every metrics build (that is what makes them a tripwire), but the curves are fitted from
			// the phases themselves and from the total, both of which are already here.
			continue
		end 'isUnattributed' else 'unknownKind'
			throw ScaleError.reason("metrics row has an unknown kind '{kind}': {trimmed}")
		end 'unknownKind'
	end 'eachLine'

	if not totalSeen 'noTotal'
		throw ScaleError.reason("metrics for rung {rung.index} carried no `total` row")
	end 'noTotal'
	if not codeSeen 'noCode'
		throw ScaleError.reason("metrics for rung {rung.index} carried no `code` row — the corpus self-check cannot run without it")
	end 'noCode'

	for phase in CompilePhase.allCases 'checkSeen'
		let seen = try phaseSeen.get(phase.ordinal) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: phaseSeen.get OOB for '{phase.name}'")
		if not seen 'missing'
			throw ScaleError.reason("metrics for rung {rung.index} never reported phase '{phase.name}' — a phase added to CompilePhase but not to the metrics output would silently vanish from every curve and every trend column")
		end 'missing'
	end 'checkSeen'

	return RungResult.create(rung.index, sourceBytes: rung.sourceBytes, codeBytes: codeBytes, total: total, phases: phases, regAlloc: regAlloc)
end 'parseRungMetrics'

function parseMetricFields(fields StringArray) returns PhaseMetric throws ScaleError
	let allocs = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsAllocsField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: allocs field OOB after count check"))
	let frees = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsFreesField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: frees field OOB after count check"))
	let bytes = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsBytesField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: bytes field OOB after count check"))
	let cpuTicks = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsCpuTicksField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: cpuTicks field OOB after count check"))
	return PhaseMetric.create(allocs, frees: frees, bytes: bytes, cpuTicks: cpuTicks)
end 'parseMetricFields'

export typealias ParsedCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// A non-negative decimal integer — THE one decimal parser in the suite (the metrics TSV, the trend
// log's grouped digits, `--rungs`, and the trace's `size=` fields all come through here). Every
// number any of them carries is a count, a byte volume, or a nanosecond reading, and none of them can
// be negative.
//
// The digit-count guard is not decoration: `--rungs=999999999999999999999` is user input, and
// without it the accumulator would run past `u64.max` and trap on the range rather than being
// rejected as the nonsense it is. Twenty digits is `u64.max`'s own width.
export function parseCount(raw String) returns ParsedCount throws ScaleError
	if raw.isEmpty() 'empty'
		throw ScaleError.reason("expected a number, got an empty field")
	end 'empty'

	let digits = raw.toByteArray()
	if digits.count() > MaxDecimalDigits 'tooManyDigits'
		throw ScaleError.reason("'{raw}' has {digits.count()} digits — no count in this suite exceeds {MaxDecimalDigits}")
	end 'tooManyDigits'

	var value = 0 as ParsedCount
	for digit in digits 'eachDigit'
		if digit < AsciiDigitZero or digit > AsciiDigitNine 'notADigit'
			throw ScaleError.reason("expected a number, got '{raw}'")
		end 'notADigit'
		value = value * 10 + (digit - AsciiDigitZero)
	end 'eachDigit'
	return value
end 'parseCount'

// The decimal width of u64.max — one more digit than any value the accumulator above can hold.
let MaxDecimalDigits = 20

let AsciiDigitZero = 0x30
let AsciiDigitNine = 0x39

// ===========================================================================================
// Reporting — data, and nothing but
// ===========================================================================================

let RungColumnWidth = 10 as ColumnWidth
let SizeColumnWidth = 12 as ColumnWidth
let CountColumnWidth = 14 as ColumnWidth
let GrowthColumnWidth = 9 as ColumnWidth

// The per-phase table's columns. `phase` is wide enough for `phase:elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
let PhaseColumnWidth = 28 as ColumnWidth
let PhaseCountColumnWidth = 12 as ColumnWidth

// THE LADDER'S OWN TABLE, and the `x` columns are what make it readable at a glance.
//
// Each rung DOUBLES the program, so a column of x2.00 is a compiler that is linear in its input and
// a column of x4.00 is one that is quadratic. Nothing is fitted and nothing is interpreted: the
// number printed is this rung's count divided by the one below it.
function printRungHeader()
	print("\n")
	print("{padRight("  rung", width: RungColumnWidth)}{padLeft("source", width: SizeColumnWidth)}{padLeft("code", width: SizeColumnWidth)}{padLeft("allocs", width: CountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("x", width: GrowthColumnWidth)}{padLeft("frees", width: CountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("bytes", width: CountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("x", width: GrowthColumnWidth)}\n")
end 'printRungHeader'

// `results` is the rungs climbed SO FAR, not including this one — the line is printed as each rung
// lands, so that a run whose top rung dies has still told you everything the rungs below it said.
//
// Every table row below is accumulated in a `StringBuilder`, not a `String`: a `String` owns exactly
// the bytes it holds, so each `append` recopies the whole row so far (stdlib/String.maxon), and the
// phase table's rows carry one column per rung.
function printRungLine(result RungResult, results RungResultArray)
	var line = StringBuilder.create()
	line.append(padRight("  rung {result.rung}", width: RungColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.sourceBytes}", width: SizeColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.codeBytes}", width: SizeColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.total.allocs}", width: CountColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft(growthText(growthAgainstPrevious(result.total.allocs, results: results, measure: ScaleMeasure.allocations)), width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.total.frees}", width: CountColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.total.bytes}", width: CountColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft(growthText(growthAgainstPrevious(result.total.bytes, results: results, measure: ScaleMeasure.bytes)), width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	print("{line.build()}\n")
end 'printRungLine'

// This rung's growth over the LAST rung in `results` — which is the rung below it, because the
// caller has not pushed this one yet. Rung 0 has nothing below it and says so.
function growthAgainstPrevious(now MeasuredQuantity, results RungResultArray, measure ScaleMeasure) returns GrowthReading
	if results.count() == 0 'bottomRung'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'bottomRung'

	let below = try results.get(results.count() - 1) otherwise panic("growthAgainstPrevious: count > 0 but the last result is missing")
	return growthOf(now, previous: measureOf(below.total, measure: measure))
end 'growthAgainstPrevious'

// WHICH of a phase's published measures a table is about. ONE value threaded through one accessor,
// rather than a copy of every table function per measure differing only in the field it reads — the
// allocations table, the bytes table and the CPU table are the same table asked a different question.
//
// It was a `bool` while there were exactly two. A third measure is precisely the moment a boolean
// stops being able to say what it means, so it became an enum rather than growing a second flag —
// two bools would encode four states for three answers, and nothing would say which fourth.
//
// FREES ARE NOT A TABLE HERE. They track allocations almost exactly (a compile that allocates N and
// frees N-k has k live at exit, and k is small and constant), so a copy of this table would be a
// near-duplicate paid for in a screenful. They are measured, printed per rung above, and carried
// per phase in `--result-json` for anyone who wants them.
export enum ScaleMeasure
	allocations
	bytes
	// ⚠ The one measure here that is NOT bit-reproducible — see PhaseMetric's header. It is the only
	// one that can see a cost which allocates nothing, which is why it is worth its noise band.
	cpuTicks
end 'ScaleMeasure'

export function measureOf(metric PhaseMetric, measure ScaleMeasure) returns MeasuredQuantity
	return match measure 'm'
		allocations gives metric.allocs
		bytes gives metric.bytes
		cpuTicks gives metric.cpuTicks
	end 'm'
end 'measureOf'

// EVERY PHASE, AT EVERY RUNG — the table that localizes a movement.
//
// The rung table above says the compiler's allocations moved. This one says WHICH PHASE'S did, and
// the `x` column says whether that phase is growing linearly with the program or faster than it. That
// is the whole diagnostic loop: the total moved (rung table) → this phase moved (this table) → this
// TYPE, from this FUNCTION (`--per-type`).
//
// The regalloc sub-phases are printed under the phases, not among them: they are a BREAKDOWN of
// `phase:regalloc` and adding them to the same list would double-count every allocation they make.
function printPhaseTable(results RungResultArray, measure ScaleMeasure, repeat RepeatCount)
	print("\n")
	print("  per-phase {measureHeading(measure)}, and its growth over the rung below (the ladder DOUBLES: x2.00 is linear)\n")
	print("  {repeatNote(measure, repeat: repeat)}\n")
	print("\n")

	var header = StringBuilder.create()
	header.append(padRight("  phase", width: PhaseColumnWidth))
	for result in results 'eachRung'
		header.append(padLeft("rung {result.rung}", width: PhaseCountColumnWidth))
	end 'eachRung'
	header.append(padLeft("x", width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	print("{header.build()}\n")

	for source in scalePhaseSources() 'eachPhase'
		printPhaseRow(source, results: results, measure: measure)
	end 'eachPhase'
end 'printPhaseTable'

function measureHeading(measure ScaleMeasure) returns String
	return match measure 'm'
		allocations gives "ALLOCATIONS"
		bytes gives "BYTES"
		cpuTicks gives "CPU TICKS (this thread only — not reproducible to the digit; see the header)"
	end 'm'
end 'measureHeading'

// WHICH COMPILE THE COLUMN CAME FROM — printed under EVERY table, not under the CPU one alone.
//
// The rung was compiled `--repeat` times and the two kinds of column took opposite things from
// that: the exact ones took the first and re-checked the rest, the CPU one took the smallest (see
// this file's header). A reader told neither will assume whichever they last read about, and the
// two carry very different licences to act on a small movement.
function repeatNote(measure ScaleMeasure, repeat RepeatCount) returns String
	return match measure 'm'
		allocations gives exactColumnNote(repeat)
		bytes gives exactColumnNote(repeat)
		cpuTicks gives leastCpuNote(repeat)
	end 'm'
end 'repeatNote'

function exactColumnNote(repeat RepeatCount) returns String
	if repeat == 1 'oneCompile'
		return "from ONE compile per rung (--repeat=1). Exact and reproducible; a repeat would only re-check it."
	end 'oneCompile'
	return "from the FIRST of {repeat} compiles per rung, and IDENTICAL in every one of them — checked, not assumed."
end 'exactColumnNote'

function leastCpuNote(repeat RepeatCount) returns String
	if repeat == 1 'oneCompile'
		return "from ONE compile per rung (--repeat=1): a single sample, undefended against a stall."
	end 'oneCompile'
	return "the LEAST of {repeat} compiles per rung — the least-disturbed sample, since noise can only ADD to a CPU reading."
end 'leastCpuNote'

function printPhaseRow(source PhaseSource, results RungResultArray, measure ScaleMeasure)
	var line = StringBuilder.create()
	line.append(padRight("  {phaseSourceName(source)}", width: PhaseColumnWidth))

	for result in results 'eachRung'
		line.append(padLeft("{measureOf(phaseMetricOf(source, result: result), measure: measure)}", width: PhaseCountColumnWidth))
	end 'eachRung'

	line.append(padLeft(growthText(topRungGrowth(source, results: results, measure: measure)), width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	print("{line.build()}\n")
end 'printPhaseRow'

// The growth of the TOP rung over the one below it — the steepest, most informative end of the
// ladder, and the one a phase that has gone superlinear shows it in first.
function topRungGrowth(source PhaseSource, results RungResultArray, measure ScaleMeasure) returns GrowthReading
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	let top = try results.get(results.count() - 1) otherwise panic("topRungGrowth: the ladder is not empty")
	let below = try results.get(results.count() - 2) otherwise panic("topRungGrowth: the ladder has at least two rungs")

	return growthOf(measureOf(phaseMetricOf(source, result: top), measure: measure), previous: measureOf(phaseMetricOf(source, result: below), measure: measure))
end 'topRungGrowth'

// What the compiler allocated, against what it allocated when the trend log was last written.
//
// THIS IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE INSTRUMENT. These numbers are bit-for-bit reproducible AT ONE PATH, so
// a difference here is REAL — the same input through the same compiler cannot produce a different one by
// accident. It is worth explaining rather than merely noticing.
//
// ⚠ AND NEITHER COLUMN IS COMPARABLE ACROSS CHECKOUTS — they are spoiled DIFFERENTLY, which is the one
// asymmetry this report has to carry. The byte term GROWS with the program, so against a row minted
// elsewhere the byte delta is WITHHELD rather than printed. The allocation term is a CONSTANT at every
// rung, so that delta IS printed — a flat offset cancels out of every ratio and out of any delta that
// grows per rung, so its shape survives — under a caveat that its constant term does not.
//
// ScaleHistory's header holds the readings. They appear in exactly one other place — the paragraph
// `printMintedElsewhereReason` PRINTS, which is operator-facing and has to carry its own numbers — and
// no comment anywhere restates them. That is not tidiness: the claim these paragraphs replaced was
// written down in four files, so correcting it meant four hand-edits and three chances to leave one
// saying `path-independent`.
function printMemoryTrend(results RungResultArray, trend LoggedMemory, mintedAt String, bytes ByteDelta, moved bool)
	print("\n")
	match trend 'm'
		absent then printNoPreviousRun()
		present(date, _, note, allocs, loggedBytes) then printMemoryDelta(results, date: date, note: note, loggedAllocs: allocs, loggedBytes: loggedBytes, mintedAt: mintedAt, bytes: bytes, moved: moved)
	end 'm'
end 'printMemoryTrend'

function printNoPreviousRun()
	print("  {OptimizationLogRelativePath} has no rows yet — this run is the first datapoint, so there is\n")
	print("  nothing to difference it against. Record it with --note=\"<what changed and why>\".\n")
end 'printNoPreviousRun'

let TrendRungColumnWidth = 8 as ColumnWidth
let TrendCountColumnWidth = 16 as ColumnWidth
let TrendDeltaColumnWidth = 14 as ColumnWidth

// `moved` is a PARAMETER, not a local: it is `anyMemoryMoved`'s answer, which the recording sentence also
// reads. This function used to accumulate its own, which made "did the memory move" — including the rule
// that a withheld column has not — a decision written twice in two files.
function printMemoryDelta(results RungResultArray, date String, note String, loggedAllocs AllocCountArray, loggedBytes ByteCountArray, mintedAt String, bytes ByteDelta, moved bool)
	print("  vs the last row of {OptimizationLogRelativePath} — {date}, \"{note}\"\n")
	print("\n")
	print("{padRight("  rung", width: TrendRungColumnWidth)}{padLeft("allocs", width: TrendCountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("delta", width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth)}{padLeft("bytes", width: TrendCountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("delta", width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth)}\n")

	for i in 0 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		let result = try results.get(i) otherwise panic("printMemoryDelta: i < count but results.get({i}) failed")

		var line = StringBuilder.create()
		line.append(padRight("  {result.rung}", width: TrendRungColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(groupDigits(result.total.allocs), width: TrendCountColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(countDeltaText(result.total.allocs, logged: loggedAllocs, rung: i), width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(groupDigits(result.total.bytes), width: TrendCountColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(byteDeltaText(result.total.bytes, logged: loggedBytes, rung: i, bytes: bytes), width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth))
		print("{line.build()}\n")
	end 'eachRung'

	printMintedElsewhere(bytes, mintedAt: mintedAt)

	if not moved 'unchanged'
		// "every rung COMPARED", because a rung the logged row never climbed prints `-` above and was not
		// compared at all — claiming it matches would be the withheld column's mistake in the other column.
		print("\n  {unchangedColumnsText(bytes)} — every rung compared against the last logged run matches it exactly.\n")
		return
	end 'unchanged'

	// "AT ONE PATH" IS THE WHOLE QUALIFIER, and it is three words rather than a paragraph because the
	// paragraph is already printed directly above by `printMintedElsewhere` — on exactly the runs where
	// the qualifier bites. Without it this sentence tells a reader that a cross-checkout delta must be
	// the compiler, which is the attribution it was measured NOT to license.
	print("\n  A number here MOVED, and it moved for a reason: these counts are bit-for-bit reproducible,\n")
	print("  so the same input through the same compiler AT ONE PATH cannot produce a different one by\n")
	print("  accident. Explain it, and record it with --note — the instrument sees WHAT moved and never why.\n")
end 'printMemoryDelta'

// WHAT THE TABLE ABOVE IS WORTH WHEN THE TWO ROWS WERE MINTED ELSEWHERE — printed under it, and only
// when they were. One column is blank and the other is not, and a reader is owed both halves of why.
//
// The alternative was the confident wrong number this replaced, and the alternative to THAT would be a
// blank column with no explanation, which reads as a bug in the tool. A reader who is refused a
// subtraction is owed the reason and the way to get it: measure both binaries at ONE path.
function printMintedElsewhere(bytes ByteDelta, mintedAt String)
	match bytes 'b'
		comparable then return
		withheld(why) then printMintedElsewhereReason(why, mintedAt: mintedAt)
	end 'b'
end 'printMintedElsewhere'

// ⚠ THE SECOND PARAGRAPH REPLACED A FALSE ONE, and the false one was this instrument's own sentence
// about itself: "the ALLOCATION delta above is unaffected — path-independent in every A/B measured so
// far". It was not. Saying LESS than the truth here would be the same defect: the shape of that column
// really does survive, and withholding it too would throw away a reading that is sound.
function printMintedElsewhereReason(why String, mintedAt String)
	print("\n  ⚠ The BYTE delta is WITHHELD, not zero: {why}.\n")
	print("    A compile allocates the path strings it works with: it derives every stdlib source path from\n")
	print("    the COMPILER's own path, and resolves every rung source path against the WORKING DIRECTORY. So\n")
	print("    the byte column carries a term proportional to each of their LENGTHS — measured at ~2,100 bytes\n")
	print("    per character at rung 0 rising to ~9,300 at rung 5, which is not a constant a reader could\n")
	print("    subtract. Nothing is normalised here: the counts are exactly what was measured, and the one\n")
	print("    subtraction that cannot be honest is not performed.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  ⚠ The ALLOCATION delta above is NOT clean either — it is spoiled DIFFERENTLY, and the difference\n")
	print("    is what SURVIVES. Its path term is a CONSTANT, the same number at every rung, where the byte\n")
	print("    term grows with the program. So the SHAPE of that column stands — a flat offset cancels out of\n")
	print("    every rung-over-rung ratio and out of any delta that GROWS per rung — while a FLAT per-rung\n")
	print("    delta between these two rows is unattributable and must not be filed as a cost.\n")
	print("    ⇒ AND EQUALISING THE PATH LENGTH IS NOT THE FIX: measured on ONE byte-identical binary, two\n")
	print("    45-character checkouts of different DEPTH read +273 allocations at every one of six rungs,\n")
	print("    while a 45- and a 14-character checkout of the same depth read 0 at every one.\n")
	print("    ⇒ Both columns take the SAME remedy: run both binaries from ONE directory, swapped into one\n")
	print("    location. This run was minted at `{mintedAt}`.\n")
end 'printMintedElsewhereReason'

// A rung this run climbed that the logged row did not have is not a delta of zero; it is not a delta
// at all. (`assertRecordable` refuses to LOG such a run, but a --rungs=3 run with no --note is a
// perfectly reasonable thing to do while iterating, and it still prints.)
function countDeltaText(current AllocCount, logged AllocCountArray, rung LadderIndex) returns String
	let before = try logged.get(rung) otherwise 'notLogged'
		return NoBaselineText
	end 'notLogged'
	return signedDeltaText(current, before: before)
end 'countDeltaText'

// The byte column's delta cell, which is the ONE cell of this table that can be refused.
//
// `WithheldDeltaText` rather than `NoBaselineText`, and they must not look alike: "the logged run never
// climbed this rung" and "these two rows were measured against different yardsticks" are different
// facts, and a reader who cannot tell them apart learns neither.
function byteDeltaText(current ByteCount, logged ByteCountArray, rung LadderIndex, bytes ByteDelta) returns String
	return match bytes 'b'
		withheld gives WithheldDeltaText
		comparable gives countDeltaText(current, logged: logged, rung: rung)
	end 'b'
end 'byteDeltaText'

// Short enough that `padLeft` still puts space in front of it at `TrendDeltaColumnWidth` — a cell that
// exactly fills its column runs into the one beside it and the table stops being a table. The ⚠ block
// under it carries the reason; this only has to be unmistakably not a number, and not `-`.
let WithheldDeltaText = "withheld"

function signedDeltaText(current Delta, before Delta) returns String
	if current > before 'grew'
		return "+{groupDigits(current - before)}"
	end 'grew'
	return groupDigits(current - before)
end 'signedDeltaText'

// A position in the ladder — an index into the parallel per-rung arrays (this run's results, and
// the logged run's columns), which is NOT the same thing as `RungIndex`: those arrays may be shorter
// than the ladder the log recorded, and that is exactly the case the delta report has to handle.
export typealias LadderIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

// ⭐ WHAT THE COMPILER'S OWN SOURCE WALK ACTUALLY TOUCHED — the files it found and the directories it
// listed, under the `stdlib/` every rung of this run compiles against.
//
// ⚠ **GIT CANNOT SEE IGNORED CONTENT AND THIS WALK CAN, so two checkouts of ONE COMMIT need not be one
// tree.** `collectMaxonSources` recurses into every subdirectory of `stdlib/`, dot-directories
// included, and `stdlib/` is walked once per compile — so a gitignored directory present in the main
// checkout and absent from every `git worktree add` adds a FLAT constant to `phase:load` at every rung,
// which is exactly the shape a real code change is read as. One did: an empty `stdlib/.maxon`, v1's
// orphaned cache, for weeks (PLAN.md A2y). It cost two separate investigations and it put a WRONG
// CONCLUSION in the trend log before anyone found it.
//
// ⭐ **THE COUNT IS HERE BECAUSE THE DELTA CANNOT BE DECODED WITHOUT IT.** An operator staring at a flat
// +12 allocations cannot turn that into "one stray directory" without already knowing this host's
// per-directory cost — which is the bisect the number is supposed to replace. And that cost is neither
// portable nor constant: measured at 12 allocations / 4,879 bytes per stray directory on arm64-macOS
// against A2y's 14 / 829 on its own host, because `Directory.list` is `FindFirstFile` on Windows and
// `opendir`/`readdir` here, and because the byte column carries the path-length term
// `printMintedElsewhereReason` already documents. The COUNT is the only signal that survives the move.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RULE THIS IS ONE HALF OF: AN A/B ACROSS TWO CHECKOUTS IS NOT SOUND BY DEFAULT.** Two known
// ways it goes wrong are now named by this instrument, and BOTH produce a FLAT per-rung delta — which is
// precisely the shape of a real per-compile cost, and is why each was read as one:
//
//   - the TREE differs, because git cannot see ignored content (A2y, above — this census);
//   - the PATH differs, because a compile allocates the path strings it works with (A3p — see
//     ScaleHistory's header for the readings, and `printMintedElsewhereReason` for what it prints).
//
// They are separate mechanisms with separate remedies, so they are detected separately; what they share
// is the reading they corrupt. ⇒ A FLAT delta between two checkouts is the one shape to distrust, and a
// delta that GROWS per rung is the one neither mechanism can fake.
//
// It is a number a reader COMPARES against the checkout a logged row was minted at, and nothing more.
// No threshold, no baseline, no refusal — this instrument renders no verdicts (see this file's header),
// and a census that refused would be the deleted gate apparatus growing back.
//
// The one thing that IS a refusal is being unable to walk at all, which is a BROKEN RUN in the ordinary
// sense: every rung compile resolves stdlib the same way and would fail the same way, so saying it here
// beats reporting it as "rung 0 did not deliver a rung to measure".
function printStdlibWalkCensus() throws ScaleError
	// The same resolution every spawned rung compile makes — this runner IS the compiler it spawns
	// (`compilerExe` is its own path), so asking it here asks about the very directory they will load.
	let stdlibDir = try locateStdlibDir() otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("stdlib/ could not be located by walking up from the running compiler's own path — every rung would fail to load it")

	var sources = MaxonSourcePathArray.create()
	let directories = try collectMaxonSources(stdlibDir, into: sources) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("{stdlibDir.toString()} could not be listed")

	print("{padRight(StdlibWalkLabel, width: StdlibWalkLabelWidth)}{groupDigits(sources.count())} source file(s) in {groupDigits(directories as Delta)} directory(ies) under {stdlibDir.toString()}\n")
	print("{padRight("", width: StdlibWalkLabelWidth)}git cannot see IGNORED content and this walk can, so compare these two counts against\n")
	print("{padRight("", width: StdlibWalkLabelWidth)}the checkout a logged row was minted at before reading a flat delta as a code change.\n")
end 'printStdlibWalkCensus'

// Indented to the same column as every other line of the report. The label carries no trailing space
// of its own: the width is what separates it from the counts AND what the two continuation lines are
// padded to, so the label and its hanging indent cannot drift apart.
let StdlibWalkLabel = "  stdlib walk:"
let StdlibWalkLabelWidth = 15 as ColumnWidth

// The corpus self-check's own numbers. They are PRINTED, not merely consulted: a reader looking at a
// set of tidy proportional memory numbers is entitled to see the evidence that they are numbers about
// a program that was actually compiled, rather than one that folded away to nothing.
function printCorpusGrowth(results RungResultArray)
	print("\n")
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		print("  corpus: {results.count()} rung(s) — too few to say anything about GROWTH, this check included.\n")
		return
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	var line = StringBuilder.create()
	line.append("  corpus: emitted code grows")
	for i in 1 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		line.append(" {growthText(codeGrowthAt(results, rung: i))}")
	end 'eachRung'

	print("{line.build()} per rung (floor {growthText(GrowthReading.measured(MinCodeGrowthRatio))}) —\n")
	print("          the programs compiled rather than folding away, so the numbers above are numbers\n")
	print("          about something.\n")
end 'printCorpusGrowth'
